**Read:** [Assessment of Treatment Plant Performance and Water Quality Data — von Sperling, Verbyla and Oliveira](../sources/Treatment-Plant-Performance-and-Water-Quality-Data.lingua#r_5PZG8VN422GHD657FBDR5KRWGS), chapter 4 in full; [Industrial Water Treatment Operation and Maintenance — UFC 3-230-13](../sources/Industrial-Water-Treatment-UFC-3-230-13.lingua#r_41KNKBCA6GZWQ6VAJGAPG2HJ3R), chapter 7 on sampling and testing; [Analytical Chemistry 2.1 — David Harvey](../../../../stem/chemistry/general-chemistry/sources/Analytical-Chemistry-Harvey.lingua#r_0XMY75E3FRXRGR3BK28V3JAC0T), the selected method and quality-assurance discussion.

Temperature, pH, conductivity, turbidity, dissolved oxygen and disinfectant residual are measured by different physical or chemical methods. Each method has a calibration, response time, range and interference problem. A continuously displayed number can be precise while representing the wrong location or a fouled sensor.

Separate instrument calibration from the complete sampling and analytical procedure. Consider line flushing, gas exchange, suspended solids, temperature compensation and chemical changes before analysis. Compare a field measurement with a laboratory result only after reconciling their sample histories and reporting bases.

For one operational instrument and one laboratory method, reconstruct the measurement chain from the water to the reported result. Identify an independent check and a realistic fault that the routine display would not reveal. Connect the uncertainty to the decision margin rather than reporting extra digits as evidence of accuracy.

